=== Data Structures ===

For platforms where STL is unavailable or undesirable, Rudiments provides a set of commonly used data structures.

* '''String Storage'''
 * [programming/stringbuffer.html Using the stringbuffer class]
* '''Binary Data Storage'''
 * [programming/bytebuffer.html Using the bytebuffer class]
 * [programming/memorypool.html Using the memorypool class]
* '''Containers'''
 * [programming/dynamicarray.html Using the dynamicarray class]
 * [programming/staticcarray.html Using the staticcarray class]
 * [programming/linkedlist.html Using the linkedlist class]
 * [programming/singlylinkedlist.html Using the singlylinkedlist class]
 * [programming/dictionary.html Using the dictionary class]

The Rudiments data structures arguably have some advantages over their STL counterparts too.

* Assignments to staticarray and dynamicarray are more intuitive than std::array and std::vector assignments.
* Unlike std::forward_list, the singlylinkedlist class doesn't require C++11.
* bytebuffer is far less clumsy than using std::string or std::vector<unsigned char> to store binary data.
* The dictionary class uses keys and values directly rather than requiring the std::pair intermediary.

